   "statement": "<p>The scoreboard should show the biggest numbers at the top. Sort the list from <strong>largest to smallest</strong> using bubble sort — think about which comparison decides when two neighbours swap.</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. The second line holds <code>n</code> integers.</p>",
   "output": "<p>The <code>n</code> integers in descending order, separated by single spaces.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 1000; −10<sup>6</sup> ≤ every number ≤ 10<sup>6</sup>.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "6\n3 -1 4 1 -5 9\n",
     "out": "9 4 3 1 -1 -5\n"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>In ascending bubble sort you swap when the left neighbour is bigger. For descending order, swap when it is <em>smaller</em>.</p>",
   "solution": "n = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\nfor i in range(n - 1):\n    for j in range(n - 1 - i):\n        if a[j] < a[j + 1]:\n            a[j], a[j + 1] = a[j + 1], a[j]\nprint(*a)",
