   "slug": "top-students",
   "title": "Top Five Students",
   "level": 2,
   "statement": "<p>Students are numbered 1 to <code>n</code> in the order of the input, and each has three scores: maths, English and science. Rank them by <strong>total</strong> score, highest first. If two totals are equal, the higher <strong>maths</strong> score ranks higher; if those are equal too, the smaller student number ranks higher. Print the top five (or everyone, if there are fewer than five).</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. Line <code>i + 1</code> holds the three scores of student <code>i</code>.</p>",
   "output": "<p>Up to five lines, each with a student number and that student's total, best first.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 300; every score is between 0 and 100.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "6\n90 80 70\n85 85 70\n100 60 60\n70 90 90\n85 80 75\n60 60 60\n",
     "out": "4 250\n1 240\n2 240\n5 240\n3 220\n",
     "why": "Students 1, 2 and 5 all total 240. Student 1 has the best maths (90); 2 and 5 tie on maths too, so 2 comes first."
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>Store each student as <code>(number, total, maths)</code>. Then \"<code>x</code> comes after <code>y</code>\" is: smaller total; or same total and smaller maths; or both equal and bigger number.</p>",
   "solution": "def after(x, y):\n    if x[1] != y[1]:\n        return x[1] < y[1]\n    if x[2] != y[2]:\n        return x[2] < y[2]\n    return x[0] > y[0]\n\n\nn = int(input())\nst = []\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    m, e, s = map(int, input().split())\n    st.append((i, m + e + s, m))\nfor i in range(n - 1):\n    for j in range(n - 1 - i):\n        if after(st[j], st[j + 1]):\n            st[j], st[j + 1] = st[j + 1], st[j]\nfor num, total, maths in st[:5]:\n    print(num, total)",
