   "slug": "most-meetings",
   "title": "As Many Meetings as Possible",
   "level": 3,
   "statement": "<p>There are <code>n</code> meeting requests for one room. Request <code>i</code> wants the room from time <code>s</code> to time <code>e</code>. Two accepted meetings may not overlap, but one may start at the very moment another ends. What is the largest number of requests you can accept?</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. Each of the next <code>n</code> lines holds <code>s e</code>.</p>",
   "output": "<p>The largest number of meetings that can be accepted.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 1000; 0 ≤ s &lt; e ≤ 10<sup>6</sup>.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "5\n1 4\n3 5\n0 6\n5 7\n8 9\n",
     "out": "3\n",
     "why": "For example 1–4, 5–7 and 8–9."
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>Which meeting should you accept first? Not the shortest and not the earliest-starting one, but the one that <em>ends</em> earliest: it leaves the most room for the rest. Sort by end time and be greedy.</p>",
   "solution": "n = int(input())\nm = []\nfor _ in range(n):\n    s, e = map(int, input().split())\n    m.append((e, s))\nfor i in range(n - 1):\n    k = i\n    for j in range(i + 1, n):\n        if m[j] < m[k]:\n            k = j\n    m[i], m[k] = m[k], m[i]\ncount = 0\nfree_at = -1\nfor e, s in m:\n    if s >= free_at:\n        count += 1\n        free_at = e\nprint(count)",
   "explanation": "<p>Swapping argument: take any best schedule. Its first meeting ends no earlier than the meeting that ends earliest overall, so replacing it with that one still works. Repeat the same idea for the rest of the day.</p>",
